# playpick cell: mode=clear spec=rogue:assassination style=auto size=25
# difficulty=heroic view=844x390,touch save=carried (behaves as fresh, #273 --
# carried never actually carries across separate playbot invocations because
# the dev-server port is derived from the process pid).
#
# A fresh/carried save only opens Bonegrinder 10-normal by default, so
# #b=marrow&s=25&h=1 unlocks the whole chain up to 25-heroic before backing
# out to a real RAID -> WALK IN at the door (direction.md's 2026-09-26
# driver-lesson recipe), rather than landing on the invite's own single-fight
# "visiting" framing.
#
# What no session has done before: no `mode=clear` evening has ever been run
# under `style=auto` (checked playtest/sessions.jsonl -- eleven prior `clear`
# entries, none `auto`; the two prior `auto` evenings on record were both
# `mode=walk` coverage-label runs, not the full door-to-as-far-as-it-goes
# walk). This is also the first time `auto` has been given rogue:assassination,
# the fastest class in the roster (moveSpeed 178, direction.md's #6 line), on
# a touch viewport where the autocast toggle is actually reachable (every
# prior `auto` session on a desktop viewport silently fell back to `good`).
# direction.md already knows from source that `auto`'s own steering in
# scripts/playbot.ts never sets a `want` vector at all -- the body just stands
# wherever it lands -- and the one prior melee-class `auto` reading (druid:
# feral, mode=walk) showed a body parked ~207 units off the boss dealing
# next to nothing. This evening asks whether that holds for a full multi-room
# walk rather than a single fight, and whether rogue's own speed (which
# crosses every corridor clean regardless of style, per #6) changes anything
# once the corridor's own steering hands off to the fight's total absence of it.
open #b=marrow&s=25&h=1
tap back
tap raid
tap next
tap class:rogue:assassination
tap pull
waitscreen fight 15
state
shot start

evening auto 200 6
state
shot end
says
bill
